With me today, Dmitry Gurski, CEO and co-founder of Flow Health.

10
00:01:02,240 --> 00:01:19,280
On the podcast, I talk with Dmitry about why the Big Mac index failils for app pricing, how Spotify and Netflix are paving the way for subscriptions globally, and why lower prices may be the key to unlocking profitable user acquisition in emerging markets.

11
00:01:19,680 --> 00:01:22,400
Hey Dimitri, thank you so much for joining me on the podcast today.

12
00:01:22,560 --> 00:01:23,120
Hello, David.

13
00:01:23,520 --> 00:01:24,320
Thank you for inviting.

14
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:28,640
I'm really excited to talk to you specifically about regional pricing.

15
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:39,840
It's something we're going to be sharing stats on in the 2025 status of subscription apps report, and it's something I don't think many apps have really cracked, but Flow is one of the few apps I've seen that does a really good job.

16
00:01:41,680 --> 00:01:42,800
at regional pricing.

17
00:01:43,120 --> 00:01:45,600
So at a high level, how do you think about it?

18
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:49,760
What has kind of guided your strategy for regional pricing around the world?

19
00:01:50,640 --> 00:01:52,080
There are several principles.

20
00:01:52,720 --> 00:02:05,120
My first statement will be that it's not right to use very simplistic approach, like, for example, like a Mach index or, for example, to use pricing of Netflix or Spotify.

21
00:02:05,760 --> 00:02:07,680
In most cases, it would not work.

22
00:02:08,320 --> 00:02:32,720
And, for example, why it would not work in the case of Mach index when you're comparing, like cost of a burger in the United States with cost of a burger in Brazil, the main reason that you have very different audience, like different customers for burger and potentially for your app, I may predict that average buyer customer of a burger in Brazil is just like average person in Brazil.

23
00:02:32,960 --> 00:02:43,040
But average customer of your subscription in Brazil probably is among more like more affluent part of society and probably uses iOS device rather than Android device.

24
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:45,760
And because of that, price may be much higher.

25
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:49,360
than your big market index would predict.

26
00:02:50,000 --> 00:03:02,480
Almost always prices regionally are lower than in the United States, but there is a huge difference between approach pricing for iOS for Apple devices and for Android.

27
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:14,560
If you compare like optimal pricing for iOS and Android in rich countries like the United Kingdom for example or the United States, like Android is always cheaper, almost always cheaper.

28
00:03:15,280 --> 00:03:19,360
However, not so much cheaper, maybe twenty percent cheaper, maybe fifteen cheaper.

29
00:03:19,760 --> 00:03:27,520
Regions like Brazil or difference in optimal pricing may be huge because richer parts of society use iOS devices.

30
00:03:27,920 --> 00:03:38,880
And because of that, propensity to pay and even ability to pay is extremely different between these categories of users in the English speaking world and worldwide.

31
00:03:39,200 --> 00:03:51,440
And because of that, pricing for iOS and Android regionally may have very big difference in comparison with iOS and Android in the United States or in English speaking world.

32
00:03:51,440 --> 00:03:51,680
In an English-speaking world.

33
00:03:52,080 --> 00:03:54,800
As a larger app, you get to do a ton of experimentation.

34
00:03:55,280 --> 00:04:00,480
Even in some of these countries, I think that is a struggle for a lot of apps is that they don't have the volume.

35
00:04:00,880 --> 00:04:07,200
How do you think about actually testing these prices and how do you run those tests and then how do you evaluate the winner?

36
00:04:07,920 --> 00:04:12,560
We have like a luxury have huge audience and we have huge audience worldwide.

37
00:04:12,800 --> 00:04:15,040
Like, for us, the biggest health app in the world.

38
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:19,680
And we have maybe more than two hundred thousand installs daily.

39
00:04:19,920 --> 00:04:24,080
And of course, having such audience and having seventy million massive active users.

40
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:28,000
We may afford to make ton of experiments.

41
00:04:28,240 --> 00:04:44,160
And because of that, we may make such experiments just like using a kind of brutal force approach, just like create enough testing groups, like you put many prices to test, you compare with initial price and make a decision for each region.

42
00:04:45,120 --> 00:04:52,240
But very significant moment, he considered that we are not measuring pricing and success in pricing by conversions.

43
00:04:52,720 --> 00:04:54,560
And it's very often a big mistake.

44
00:04:55,120 --> 00:04:59,120
We measure pricing by influence on average revenue per user.

45
00:04:59,280 --> 00:04:59,600
And why?

46
00:05:00,160 --> 00:05:03,760
Because pricing is related with many other metrics.

47
00:05:04,160 --> 00:05:11,280
Conversion rate to trial, then toxication in trial, then retention of subscription depends significantly on cost.

48
00:05:11,760 --> 00:05:22,880
We have situations when pricing may be significantly cheaper, for example, twice cheaper, but then retention is three times higher and overall RPU will be much higher.

49
00:05:23,040 --> 00:05:30,160
And because of that, we are making decisions not based on pricing itself or conversion, but based on influence on RPU.

50
00:05:30,560 --> 00:05:39,440
Do you have any specific examples of countries where you did price experimentation and like what the results of that price experimentation was on revenue?

51
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:52,480
We got quite significant success in Brazil this year and significant means that revenue was growing like hundreds of percent or year to year very significantly.

52
00:05:52,640 --> 00:06:00,320
And now Brazil is the number three market for us, versus maybe market number four significant market, really significant market.

53
00:06:00,720 --> 00:06:01,440
And what happened?

54
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:03,120
We reduced price.

55
00:06:04,080 --> 00:06:06,000
significantly, really significantly.

56
00:06:06,640 --> 00:06:15,600
And it helped not just increase conversions and improve retention, but also what was the result we didn't expect.

57
00:06:16,160 --> 00:06:26,640
It unlocked paid user acquisition because with this price conversions are enough to provide enough signals to networks and to get traffic profitably.

58
00:06:26,960 --> 00:06:36,480
Like we found like a hotspot for paid user acquisition with this pricing and we increased volume of paid user acquisition in Brazil very profitably significantly.

59
00:06:36,480 --> 00:06:38,640
We're going to increase profitably significantly this year because of that.

60
00:06:39,200 --> 00:06:47,840
And it would not be possible with higher prices just because like level of conversions is not enough to really start winning auctions.

61
00:06:48,480 --> 00:07:02,080
It's such a counterintuitive thing, but in a country like Brazil where the ad inventory is so cheap, then the lower prices and the lower prices giving a decent bump in conversion actually makes it work.

62
00:07:02,320 --> 00:07:03,440
So that's fascinating.

63
00:07:03,760 --> 00:07:15,280
I'm going to go out on a limit, propose that subscription apps across the entire industry should instead of using the Big Mac index, they should be using the Flow index, given how much price testing you've done.

64
00:07:15,680 --> 00:07:33,600
Do you think that your pricing and your strategy, and I would say like percentages, so if US is the index of one, and then your Brazil price is zero point two, and your Mexico price is zero point three, and your Germany price is zero point eight, that would be kind of the Flow index of pricing.

65
00:07:33,840 --> 00:07:42,080
Do you think the pricing works very specifically for flow, or do you think that what you've learned about pricing might be even more broadly applicable to the industry?

66
00:07:42,720 --> 00:07:55,040
Recently, we compared our prices in Flo with like average prices in health and fitness category and not even in absolute numbers but more kind of relatively United States.

67
00:07:55,760 --> 00:08:00,400
And I would say that it's pretty much correlated, but there are exceptions.

68
00:08:01,520 --> 00:08:45,040
And mostly these exceptions maybe not so much about the country itself, maybe more about what apps are popular and age distribution in these apps because Flo naturally has mostly users younger than 35 and other health apps they significantly sell subscriptions to people over 45 and then demographic and distribution of Android and iOS may influence average pricing a lot and because of that I would not advise using any index or any benchmark because optimal pricing may depend on many parameters like age of your users, platform, type of product, cultural specifics and it just must be tested.

69
00:08:45,360 --> 00:08:48,080
That's really fantastic advice and the cumulative.

70
00:08:48,640 --> 00:08:56,960
effect of this approach is that Flow seems to be doing much better outside the US than many subscription apps.

71
00:08:57,120 --> 00:09:02,480
I mean, so many apps I talk to are so dependent on the US and make almost nothing in other countries.

72
00:09:02,800 --> 00:09:04,000
What does that look like for Flow?

73
00:09:04,160 --> 00:09:08,480
What percentage of revenue or how are you doing outside the US compared to inside?

74
00:09:08,880 --> 00:09:15,680
It's true, and our international revenue is growing, and even this growth is speeding up.

75
00:09:16,160 --> 00:09:31,680
For example, this year, we are like revenue out of rich English-speaking world vault increased like almost 80 percent and in the English speaking vault was 35 percent and that mix was like 45 percent of revenue growth this year.

76
00:09:32,240 --> 00:09:46,960
But my prediction that long term, Flo will earn more money originally than in the United States and not even like in the English speaking vault because I also like Australia, Canada, United Kingdom like behave pretty much the same as United States.

77
00:09:47,440 --> 00:09:53,920
And now we have maybe 70 percent of our revenue from this kind of English speaking vault like what is traditional.

78
00:09:54,480 --> 00:09:56,720
source of money for subscription companies.

79
00:09:56,960 --> 00:10:03,280
But regions are growing very fast and it's much more cultural phenomenon than anything else.

80
00:10:03,600 --> 00:10:26,880
And I have a hypothesis that it's pretty much driven by efforts of huge subscription businesses like Spotify and Netflix because we see that when Spotify, Netflix and Apple, when they double down on specific markets, for example Germany or then Brazil, then several years after people there start to pay for floor subscription or other subscriptions just because they.

81
00:10:27,360 --> 00:10:32,240
get into this habit, the acceptance that it's okay to pay for digital subscriptions.

82
00:10:32,400 --> 00:10:37,600
And there are plenty of regions where people have a decent income, for example, Italy or Poland.

83
00:10:38,240 --> 00:10:43,040
And conversions to subscriptions are really low, and not just for Flo but for all companies.

84
00:10:43,360 --> 00:10:45,920
And again, not just because people there can't pay.

85
00:10:46,400 --> 00:10:52,640
It's because of cultural acceptance of the idea itself that it's okay to pay for digital products.

86
00:10:53,120 --> 00:10:54,320
Yeah, that's fascinating.

87
00:10:54,720 --> 00:10:59,920
The last thing I wanted to talk to you about was Flo is doing something really different that I've seen.

88
00:11:00,560 --> 00:11:01,680
any other app do.

89
00:11:02,320 --> 00:11:06,880
And that's that you're offering a completely free version in a lot of countries.

90
00:11:07,120 --> 00:11:10,080
Tell me about why you're doing that and how that's playing out.

91
00:11:10,400 --> 00:11:20,080
We believe that the floor is a very valuable, very useful product, and we just don't believe we have many scientific research to prove that.

92
00:11:20,800 --> 00:11:32,160
And we just think that it's not right that he blocks so useful a product behind paywall in countries where women just can't pay because of different reasons.

93
00:11:32,400 --> 00:11:47,040
because in some regions they just don't have credit cards or they just don't have money altogether and conversions to subscriptions would be really low and 99% or even more of our users would not have access to the most useful features.

94
00:11:47,440 --> 00:11:59,600
And because of our mission, we decided that it's better if you just open that for free and hundreds of millions of women worldwide will be able to become healthy and get knowledge because of law.

95
00:12:00,320 --> 00:12:06,640
And after this moment, we have given more than twenty million subscriptions, like twenty million subscripriptions.

96
00:12:06,880 --> 00:12:13,520
We gifted more subscriptions than we sold because like now we have six million active subscribers.

97
00:12:13,760 --> 00:12:27,360
Maybe this like Chad, maybe I don't know, maybe we have sold ten million or so, but we have given twenty million and twenty million women worldwide in poor countries, they may use Flo for free.

98
00:12:27,520 --> 00:12:29,280
And it's more like a business here.

99
00:12:29,600 --> 00:12:36,320
It's like our idea that if you're doing something good, then when people may pay, they should pay.

100
00:12:36,400 --> 00:12:38,480
But when people can't pay, it's almost.

101
00:12:39,200 --> 00:12:43,680
kind of sinful to hide a product behind a payable.

102
00:12:43,840 --> 00:12:48,240
And because of that, Flo is so free in more than fifty countries.

103
00:12:48,640 --> 00:12:49,760
Wow, that's incredible.

104
00:12:49,920 --> 00:12:51,920
And a great way to align the mission.

105
00:12:52,240 --> 00:12:53,920
Are there learnings that do help?

106
00:12:54,400 --> 00:12:59,520
Is there data coming from those users or other things that do help propel the business?

107
00:12:59,920 --> 00:13:02,160
Or is it just fully kind of a charity?

108
00:13:02,560 --> 00:13:03,760
No, it's just charity.

109
00:13:04,160 --> 00:13:06,880
You're not getting any business value from that.

110
00:13:07,280 --> 00:13:11,680
Like we're getting business value in the sense that our employees are more motivated.

111
00:13:12,240 --> 00:13:30,160
because Flo is a good company and our users are more motivated because of that and been informed that they are doing this pro social campaign, but there is no direct business value, like additional revenue or some additional growth we would get because of that.

112
00:13:30,320 --> 00:13:39,040
No, it's just like because of our mission and our desire not just to earn money but also to be a good company and improve this world.

113
00:13:39,520 --> 00:13:40,160
That's fantastic.

114
00:13:40,320 --> 00:13:44,080
I don't know how many other apps are so mission driven that they would be willing to do that.

115
00:13:44,640 --> 00:13:45,680
But I think it's incredible.

116
00:13:45,840 --> 00:13:50,400
I mean, for other apps there may even be things like they could do Tom's shoes kind of made this famous.

